(c) Now use the rules of logic (Associativity, de Morgan's rule, Complement rule, etc) to show that $(P \wedge Q) \Longrightarrow P$ is a tautology.
You should indicate what rule you are using at each step.
You may assume/use the absorption rule $R \vee T \equiv T$ (where $T$ denotes true).
$$\begin{aligned}
P \wedge Q \Rightarrow P & \equiv \neg(P \wedge Q) \vee P \text { (def of ⇒ ) } \\
& \equiv(\neg P \vee \neg Q) \vee P \text { (de Morgan) } \\
& \equiv(\neg Q \vee \neg P) \vee P \text { (Commut.) } \\
& \equiv \neg Q \vee(\neg P \vee P) \text { (Assoc) } \\
& \equiv \neg Q \vee T \quad \text { (Complement) } \\
& \equiv T \quad \text { (Absorption) }
\end{aligned}$$
