(3) [6 marks]

Use the Remainder Theorem to show that if $n$ is a square number then $5 \mid n-1$ or $5 \mid n$ or $5 \mid n+1$.
$n$ square so $n=k^{2}$ some $k \in \mathbb{Z}$ By Remainder Theorem $\exists q, r \in \mathbb{Z}$
$$k=5 q+r \quad 0 \leq r<4$$
$n=k^{2}=(5 q+r)^{2}=5\left(5 q^{2}+2 q r\right)+r^{2}$
If $r=0$ then $n=5\left(5 q^{2}\right)$ so $5 \vert n$
if $r=1$ then $n=5\left(5 q^{2}+2 q\right)+1$ so $5 \vert n-1$
if $r=2$ then $n=5\left(5 q^{2}+4 q\right)+4$ so $5 \vert n+1$
If $r=3$ then $n=5\left(5 q^{2}+6 q\right)+9$
$$\text { so } 5 \vert n+1$$
if $r=4$ then $n=5\left(5 q^{2}+8 q\right)+16$
$$\text { so } 5 \mid n-1 \text {. }$$
