(4) [12 marks]
(a) State Bezout's identity.

For $a, b \in \mathbb{Z}$ not both zero
$\exists u, v \in \mathbb{Z}$ such that $a u+b v=\operatorname{gcd}(a, b)$
The gcd is least positive value of $au+bv$.
(b) Use the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor $d$ of the integers 31542 and 3120 .
$$\begin{aligned}
& 31542=3120 \cdot 10+342 \\
& 3120=342 \cdot 9+42 \\
& 342=42 \cdot 8+6 \\
& 42=6 \cdot 7+0 \\
& \text { Thus gcd }=6
\end{aligned}$$
\pagebreak

(c) Find integers $u, v$ such that $31542 u+3120 v=d$
$$\begin{aligned}
6 & =342-42 \cdot 8 \\
& =342-(3120-342 \cdot 9) \cdot 8 \\
& =342 \cdot 73-3120 \cdot 8 \\
& =(31542-3120 \cdot 10) \cdot 73-3120 \cdot 8 \\
& =31542 \cdot 73-3120 \cdot 738
\end{aligned}$$
(d) Does the equation $31542 x+3120 y=2$ have integer solutions for $x, y$ ? Justify your answer.

> There are no integer solutions
> because $\gcd=6 \ \& \ 6 \nmid 2$.
